guides.body:
An event handler implements `IEventHandler<TEvent>`. There is no result to return:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    private readonly IMailer _mailer;

    public SendWelcomeEmail(IMailer mailer) => _mailer = mailer;

    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _mailer.SendAsync(@event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Any number of handlers can exist for one event — that is the point of the module. Adding a
second one is not a configuration error, unlike with commands and queries.

## Handlers must be independent

Handlers for the same event run in a defined order (direct before covariant, then `[Weight]`
descending), but you should not build on that order. Two rules follow:

- **Do not pass data between handlers.** If handler B needs what handler A produced, they are
  one operation and belong in one handler — or A should be a command that publishes the event
  B reacts to.
- **Do not assume the others succeeded.** A handler that must run only after another
  succeeded is describing a workflow, not a broadcast.

The item bag on the context does let handlers share data within one dispatch, which makes
this easy to violate. Reserve it for
[interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/interceptors) passing data to the pipeline, not
for handler-to-handler communication.

## When a handler throws

The exception propagates out of `PublishAsync`, and the
[exception stage](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors) runs. That
means one failing subscriber can fail the whole publish — and, if the publish is inside a
command handler, the command with it.

When a subscriber's failure genuinely should not fail the publisher, contain it in the
handler:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
{
    try
    {
        await _searchIndex.AddAsync(@event.Id, @event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
    }
    catch (Exception ex) when (ex is not OperationCanceledException)
    {
        _logger.LogError(ex, "Indexing {Product} failed; continuing", @event.Id);
    }
}
```

Letting cancellation through is deliberate — a cancelled dispatch should stay cancelled.

For work that must survive process failure, do not do it in the handler at all: write to an
outbox in the handler and let a separate worker do the delivery.

## Covariant handlers

A handler registered against a base type or interface receives every assignable event:

```csharp
public sealed class AppendToOutbox : IEventHandler<IDomainEvent>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(IDomainEvent @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _outbox.AppendAsync(@event, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Handlers registered for the concrete event type run first, then covariant ones. This is what
lets an outbox, an audit sink or a metrics collector subscribe to a whole family of events
with a single class.

## Lifetimes

Event handlers resolve from the calling DI scope on each dispatch, like every other handler.
A handler holding a `DbContext` should be **scoped**. See
[Configuring Ergosfare](/ergosfare.docs/getting-started/configuring#handler-lifetimes).

Because a publish may fan out to many handlers, this is the module where transient handlers
allocate most visibly — one instance per handler per dispatch. Register them as singletons
when they are stateless.

## Publishing from a handler

Use a [nested scope](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/nested-dispatch) so the inner dispatch gets
a clean item bag and the parent's cancellation token:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderPlaced @event, IExecutionContext context)
{
    using var scope = context.CreateScope();
    await _events.PublishAsync(new InvoiceRequested(@event.OrderId), scope.Context);
}
```

Next: [Modules and extensions](/ergosfare.docs/core-components/modules-extensions).

A query handler implements `IQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>`:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class GetProductHandler : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public GetProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, IExecutionContext context)
        => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

As everywhere else: `ValueTask` return, cancellation on the context, dependencies through the
constructor, and no registration call — the source generator finds it.

## One handler per query

Like commands, a query has exactly one main handler. Two registrations for the same query
type raise `MultipleHandlerFoundException` at dispatch.

## Streaming

`IStreamQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>` returns `IAsyncEnumerable<TResult>` instead:

Note the method name: streaming handlers implement **`StreamAsync`**, not `HandleAsync`, and
there is no separate cancellation-token parameter.

```csharp
public sealed class StreamProductsHandler : IStreamQueryHandler<StreamProducts, Product>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public StreamProductsHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public async IAsyncEnumerable<Product> StreamAsync(
        StreamProducts query, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        // Read what you need off the context before the first yield: from here on the
        // method body runs during the consumer's enumeration, not during the dispatch call.
        var cancellationToken = context.CancellationToken;

        await foreach (var page in _store.PagesAsync(query.PageSize, cancellationToken))
            foreach (var product in page)
                yield return product;
    }
}
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
An iterator method body does not start running until the consumer enumerates it. Capture
anything you need from `IExecutionContext` into locals before the first `yield return` rather
than touching `context` inside the loop — see
[Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/execution-context#pooling-and-the-lifetime-rule).
{% /aside %}

Consume it with `await foreach`:

```csharp
await foreach (var product in _queries.StreamAsync(new StreamProducts(100), ct))
    await WriteAsync(product);
```

## Returning "not found"

If the query is declared `IQuery<Product?>`, returning `null` is the contract and callers must
handle it. If it is declared `IQuery<Product>`, throw — and let an
[exception interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors)
translate that into whatever the edge of your application returns:

```csharp
public ValueTask<Product> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, IExecutionContext context)
    => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken)
        ?? throw new KeyNotFoundException($"Product {query.Id} not found");
```

Pick one and be consistent across the codebase; mixing the two is what makes callers write
both a null check and a try/catch.

## Caching

The pipeline is a better place for caching than the handler, because it keeps the handler
about the data:

- A [pre-interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/pre-interceptors) checks the
  cache and calls `context.Abort(cached)` on a hit, skipping the handler entirely.
- A [post-interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/post-interceptors) populates
  the cache on the way out.

Scope both to a marker interface such as `ICacheableQuery` so only the queries you opted in
pay for it.

## Lifetimes

Handlers resolve from the calling DI scope on every dispatch. A query handler that holds a
`DbContext` should be **scoped**, matching the context's own lifetime — a singleton handler
capturing a scoped dependency is the usual source of "cannot access a disposed object" at
runtime. See
[Configuring Ergosfare](/ergosfare.docs/getting-started/configuring#handler-lifetimes).

Next: [Defining an event](/ergosfare.docs/core-components/event/event-definition).

`IExecutionContext` travels with a dispatch. It carries the cancellation token, a key/value
bag for that dispatch, and the controls that let a handler or interceptor stop the pipeline.

Two properties define everything else about it:

1. **It is a parameter, never ambient.** Every handler and interceptor receives it
   explicitly. There is no `AsyncLocal`, no thread-local, and no static accessor.
2. **It is pooled.** A dispatch rents a context and returns it on completion. The context is
   valid **only for the duration of that dispatch**.

{% aside type="danger" %}
`AmbientExecutionContext` was **removed in v2**. If you are porting v1 code that reached for
it, see [Migrating from v1](/ergosfare.docs/migration/v1-to-v2). The context parameter is now
the only access path.
{% /aside %}

## What it carries

```csharp
public interface IExecutionContext
{
    CancellationToken CancellationToken { get; }
    IDictionary<object, object?> Items { get; }

    void Set(string key, object item);
    bool Has(string key);
    T Get<T>(string key) where T : notnull;
    bool TryGet<T>(string key, out T item);

    void Abort(object? messageResult = null);
    ExecutionContextScope CreateScope();
}
```

### Cancellation

```csharp
public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
    => _store.AddAsync(command.Name, command.Price, context.CancellationToken);
```

The token comes from the mediator call — either the `CancellationToken` argument or, for the
context-taking overloads, inherited from the parent context. Because it arrives on the
context rather than as a separate handler parameter, forgetting to thread it is not possible.

### The item bag

`Set`/`Get`/`Has`/`TryGet` share data along one dispatch. A pre-interceptor can stash
something the handler and post-interceptors read back:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        context.Set("started", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}

public sealed class StopTimer : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ICommand command, object? result, Exception? ex, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("started", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

`Get<T>` throws `KeyNotFoundException` if the key is absent and `InvalidCastException` if the
stored value is the wrong type. Use `TryGet<T>` when absence is expected.

You can seed the bag for a dispatch from the mediation settings:

```csharp
var settings = new CommandMediationSettings { Items = { ["tenant"] = tenantId } };
await commands.SendAsync(command, settings);
```

### Aborting

`Abort()` ends the pipeline immediately — no further handlers or interceptors run. If the
message has a result type and you abort before the main handler, you must supply the result:

```csharp
public sealed class RejectUnauthorized : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.CanCreateProducts)
            context.Abort(Guid.Empty);   // result required: the pipeline still owes one

        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

`CreateProduct` is an `ICommand<Guid>`, so the pipeline owes the caller a `Guid`. Aborting
before the main handler runs means nothing else will produce one — hence the argument. For a
command with no result, `Abort()` takes nothing.

## Pooling and the lifetime rule

Contexts are rented from a `[ThreadStatic]`-first pool — a plain load and store on the common
same-thread cycle — and returned when the dispatch completes. There is a synchronous fast
path that skips the async state machine entirely when the whole pipeline completes
synchronously.

This is where the ~2.3 MB per 100 000 dispatches comes from. It also imposes one rule:

{% aside type="danger" %}
**Never hold onto a context after its dispatch returns.** Once the dispatch completes the
context goes back to the pool and will be handed to an unrelated dispatch. Capturing it in a
field, a closure that outlives the call, or a fire-and-forget task will read another
message's state.
{% /aside %}

```csharp
// Wrong — the context is recycled the moment HandleAsync returns.
public ValueTask HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
{
    _ = Task.Run(async () =>
    {
        await Task.Delay(1000);
        context.Set("late", true);      // this context now belongs to someone else
    });
    return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
}
```

If you need something after the dispatch, copy the values out before returning.

## No service provider, on purpose

`IExecutionContext` exposes no `IServiceProvider`. Handler resolution belongs to the
dispatcher, which resolves instances from the calling DI scope. If a handler needs a service,
take it in the constructor — that keeps the dependency visible to the container and to
whoever reads the class.

## Nested dispatch

`CreateScope()` opens a child context for calling the mediator from inside a handler. It gets
its own clean item bag and inherits the parent's cancellation token. That has enough detail
to deserve its own page: [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

Next: [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

Interceptors are how cross-cutting concerns attach to a dispatch without handlers knowing
about them. They are discovered and registered exactly like handlers, and they run as part of
the same pre-built pipeline plan.

There are **four stages**:

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

- **pre** runs before the handler and may **rewrite the message**.
- **post** runs after a successful handler and may **rewrite the result**.
- **exception** runs when the handler or an interceptor threw, and may **supply a result**.
- **final** always runs last, on success and on failure alike.

Within a stage, order is `[Weight]`-descending, direct registrations before covariant ones.
See [Groups and weights](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights).

## Pre-interceptors

A pre-interceptor returns the message that the next stage will see. Returning the input
unchanged is the pass-through case:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Three arities are available, trading breadth for type safety:

| Interface | `HandleAsync` signature | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, IExecutionContext)` | Applies to every command |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` | Applies to one command type or hierarchy |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` | `ValueTask<TModified>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` | You actually rewrite the message and want it typed |

In the two-parameter form, `TModified` must derive from `TCommand`. It exists for rewriting,
so if you are only observing, use the one-parameter form:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct, CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

## Post-interceptors

A post-interceptor receives the result and returns the result the caller will get:

```csharp
public sealed class StampProduct : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, Guid result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _audit.Record(command.Name, result);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

| Interface | `HandleAsync` signature |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPostInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult>(TCommand, TResult, IExecutionContext)` |

Post-interceptors do **not** run when the pipeline threw. That is what the exception stage is
for.

## Exception interceptors

```csharp
public sealed class SwallowNotFound : ICommandExceptionInterceptor<GetOrCreate, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid?> HandleAsync(
        GetOrCreate command, Guid? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (exception is not KeyNotFoundException)
            throw exception;                 // not ours — let it propagate

        return ValueTask.FromResult<Guid?>(Guid.Empty);
    }
}
```

The interceptor receives whatever result had been produced so far (often `null`) and the
exception. Returning a value satisfies the caller; rethrowing lets the exception propagate.

## Final interceptors

Final interceptors always run — success, failure, or abort — which makes them the right place
for cleanup, metrics and audit trails. They cannot change anything:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object? result, Exception? exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("started", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(command.GetType().Name, Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started), exception);

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Note the signature: both `result` and `exception` are nullable, because exactly one of them
is meaningful on any given run.

## Coverage across modules

Every module offers all four stages, but not every arity exists everywhere — events have no
typed result, so their post and exception interceptors receive a `ValueTask` rather than a
result value.

| Stage | Commands | Queries | Events |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pre | `ICommandPreInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TModified>` | `IQueryPreInterceptor` `<TQuery>` `<TQuery, TModified>` | `IEventPreInterceptor` `<TEvent>` `<TEvent, TModified>` |
| Post | `ICommandPostInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TResult>` | `IQueryPostInterceptor` `<TQuery>` `<TQuery, TResult>` | `IEventPostInterceptor` `<TEvent>` |
| Exception | `ICommandExceptionInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TResult>` | `IQueryExceptionInterceptor` `<TQuery, TResult>` | `IEventExceptionInterceptor` `<TEvent>` |
| Final | `ICommandFinalInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TResult>` | `IQueryFinalInterceptor` `<TQuery, TResult>` | `IEventFinalInterceptor` `<TEvent>` |

The [API reference](/ergosfare.docs/api) carries the exact signature for each one.

## Covariant matching

An interceptor registered against a base type or interface applies to **every assignable
message**. This is the main reason to prefer a marker interface over a base class:

```csharp
public interface IAuditedCommand : ICommand;

public sealed class AuditTrail : ICommandPreInterceptor<IAuditedCommand>
{
    public ValueTask<IAuditedCommand> HandleAsync(IAuditedCommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _audit.Log(command);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

Every command implementing `IAuditedCommand` now goes through `AuditTrail`, with no
registration per command.

Within a stage, interceptors registered for the message type itself run **before** covariantly
matched ones. See [Polymorphism](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/polymorphism).

## Opting a message out

`[ExcludeFromPipeline]` on a *message* removes it from covariantly matched interceptors —
useful for a high-volume message you do not want the blanket logging interceptor to touch:

```csharp
[ExcludeFromPipeline]
public sealed record Heartbeat : ICommand;

[ExcludeFromPipeline("logging")]     // or just one group
public sealed record Tick : ICommand;
```

Interceptors registered for the message type *itself* always run — they were written for that
message deliberately. Main handlers are never affected.

## What interceptors are not

They are not handlers. An interceptor does not perform the operation; it observes, enriches,
short-circuits or cleans up around it. If a piece of logic is the point of the message, it
belongs in the handler.

Next: [Polymorphism](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/polymorphism).

A handler often needs to mediate further messages: reserve stock while placing an order,
publish an event after a command succeeds. Ergosfare handles this with an explicit child
scope rather than ambient state.

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(PlaceOrder msg, IExecutionContext ctx)
{
    using var scope = ctx.CreateScope();   // struct scope — allocates nothing

    var reserved = await _commands.SendAsync(new ReserveStock(msg.OrderId), scope.Context);
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(msg.OrderId), scope.Context);
}
```

## What the child gets

**Clean items.** The child starts with an empty item bag. Anything an inner interceptor
stashes stays inner — it cannot leak into the outer dispatch and confuse an outer
post-interceptor.

**The parent's cancellation token.** Inherited, not re-derived. Nested work cannot escape the
outer cancellation chain, and there is no token parameter to forget.

**Its own pipeline.** The inner message runs its own plan: its own interceptors, its own
handlers, its own abort semantics.

## Passing the child

Every mediator facade has an overload taking an `IExecutionContext`:

```csharp
ValueTask        SendAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context, CommandMediationSettings? settings = null);
ValueTask<T>     SendAsync<T>(ICommand<T> command, IExecutionContext context, CommandMediationSettings? settings = null);
ValueTask<T>     QueryAsync<T>(IQuery<T> query, IExecutionContext context, QueryMediationSettings? settings = null);
ValueTask        PublishAsync(IEvent @event, IExecutionContext context, EventMediationSettings? settings = null);
```

Pass `scope.Context`, not `ctx`. Passing the parent would run the inner dispatch on the
outer context and defeat the isolation.

## Abort is scoped too

An `Abort()` inside the child ends **only the inner pipeline**. The outer handler resumes at
the line after the inner call and decides what happens next:

```csharp
public async ValueTask<bool> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder msg, IExecutionContext ctx)
{
    using var reserve = ctx.CreateScope();
    var stock = await _commands.SendAsync(new ReserveStock(msg.OrderId), reserve.Context);

    if (!stock.Reserved)
        return false;      // inner abort did not touch this pipeline

    using var notify = ctx.CreateScope();
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(msg.OrderId), notify.Context);
    return true;
}
```

This is the part ambient-state mediators cannot offer safely. With an `AsyncLocal` context,
an inner dispatch either overwrites the ambient value — requiring a restore step that a
`finally` block has to get exactly right — or shares state with the outer pipeline. Here
there is nothing ambient to overwrite, so there is nothing to restore.

## Parallel inner dispatches

Separate scopes are independent, so fanning out is safe:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(RebuildAll msg, IExecutionContext ctx)
{
    await Task.WhenAll(msg.TenantIds.Select(async id =>
    {
        using var scope = ctx.CreateScope();
        await _commands.SendAsync(new RebuildTenant(id), scope.Context);
    }));
}
```

Each iteration rents its own child context. They share the parent's cancellation token and
nothing else.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Do not share one scope across concurrent inner dispatches. One scope means one context, and
a context is single-dispatch state.
{% /aside %}

## Lifetime

`ExecutionContextScope` is a `readonly struct` implementing `IDisposable`, and `using`
returns the pooled child. As with any pooled context, the child **must not be used after the
scope is disposed** — see
[Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/execution-context#pooling-and-the-lifetime-rule).

```csharp
// Wrong — the child is back in the pool by the time this runs.
IExecutionContext leaked;
using (var scope = ctx.CreateScope())
    leaked = scope.Context;

leaked.Set("oops", true);
```

Next: [Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/interceptors).

Ergosfare's core knows about messages, handlers, interceptors and pipeline plans. It knows
nothing about *commands*, *queries* or *events*. Those three are **modules** built on the same
extension point you can use yourself.

That is not a metaphor. `AddCommandModule` is four lines:

```csharp
public static IModuleRegistry AddCommandModule(
    this IModuleRegistry moduleRegistry,
    Action<CommandModuleBuilder> builderAction)
{
    moduleRegistry.Register(new CommandModule(builderAction));
    return moduleRegistry;
}
```

## The contract

A module is anything implementing `IModule`:

```csharp
public interface IModule
{
    void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration);
}
```

`Build` runs once, at registration time. It receives everything a module needs:

```csharp
public interface IModuleConfiguration
{
    IServiceCollection Services { get; }        // register your own services
    IMessageRegistry MessageRegistry { get; }   // register messages, handlers, interceptors
}
```

Here is the whole command module:

```csharp
internal class CommandModule : IModule
{
    private readonly Action<CommandModuleBuilder> _builder;

    public CommandModule(Action<CommandModuleBuilder> builder) => _builder = builder;

    public void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _builder(new CommandModuleBuilder(configuration.MessageRegistry));

        configuration.Services.TryAddScoped<ICommandMediator, CommandMediator>();
    }
}
```

Two responsibilities: run the user's registration lambda against a builder, and add the
module's own facade to DI. `TryAddScoped` rather than `AddScoped`, so registering a module
twice is harmless.

## Writing your own module

The case for a custom module is a *new message shape* — something that is not a command, a
query or an event, but wants the same pipeline, the same interceptor stages and the same
compile-time discovery.

```csharp
public sealed class SagaModule : IModule
{
    private readonly Action<SagaModuleBuilder> _builder;

    public SagaModule(Action<SagaModuleBuilder> builder) => _builder = builder;

    public void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _builder(new SagaModuleBuilder(configuration.MessageRegistry));

        configuration.Services.TryAddScoped<ISagaMediator, SagaMediator>();
    }
}

public static class SagaModuleRegistryExtensions
{
    public static IModuleRegistry AddSagaModule(
        this IModuleRegistry registry, Action<SagaModuleBuilder> builderAction)
    {
        registry.Register(new SagaModule(builderAction));
        return registry;
    }
}
```

Used exactly like the built-in modules:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddSagaModule(s => s.RegisterFromAssembly(typeof(MySaga).Assembly)));
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
The source generator only emits `RegisterGenerated()` for the three built-in module builders.
A custom module has to feed the registry itself — through explicit `Register<T>()` calls, its
own `RegisterFromAssembly`, or `RegisterDescriptors(...)` if you pre-compute descriptors
yourself.
{% /aside %}

## Module builders

Every builder implements the same three entry points:

```csharp
public interface IModuleBuilder
{
    IModuleBuilder Register<T>();
    IModuleBuilder Register(Type type);
    IModuleBuilder RegisterFromAssembly(Assembly assembly);
}
```

The concrete builders add module-specific overloads on top — `CommandModuleBuilder` returns
`CommandModuleBuilder` from each call so chains stay typed, and adds
`RegisterDescriptors(IEnumerable<IHandlerDescriptor>)`, which is what generated registration
uses to hand over pre-computed descriptors without reflection.

## Only take what you need

Because the modules are independent, an application that never publishes events never
registers the event module, never resolves `IEventMediator`, and never carries its assembly:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated()));   // that's it
```

The same applies at the package level — see
[Installation](/ergosfare.docs/getting-started/installation).

## The core module

`AddCoreModule` registers messages, handlers and interceptors that are not tied to any of the
three built-in shapes. It is rarely needed directly; the built-in modules already configure
what the runtime requires.

Next: [Modules and extensions](/ergosfare.docs/core-components/modules-extensions) walks
through the shipped modules in detail.

An exception interceptor runs when the handler — or anything earlier in the pipeline — threw.
It receives the message, whatever result had been produced so far, and the exception, and it
decides what the caller sees.

```csharp
public sealed class TranslateNotFound : IQueryExceptionInterceptor<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct query, Product? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (exception is KeyNotFoundException)
            return ValueTask.FromResult<Product?>(null);

        throw exception;      // not ours — let it propagate
    }
}
```

Two outcomes, and nothing in between:

- **Return a value** — the exception is considered handled, and the caller receives that value.
- **Throw** — the exception propagates. Rethrow the original when it is not yours to handle.

## Where it sits

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

Post-interceptors do not run when the pipeline threw; the exception stage takes their place.
Final interceptors run either way.

## Logging without swallowing

The common case is enriching the failure and letting it continue:

```csharp
public sealed class LogFailures : ICommandExceptionInterceptor
{
    private readonly ILogger<LogFailures> _logger;

    public LogFailures(ILogger<LogFailures> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public ValueTask<object?> HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogError(exception, "{Command} failed", command.GetType().Name);
        throw exception;
    }
}
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Returning a value from an exception interceptor **swallows the exception**. A blanket
interceptor that returns `null` on every failure will hide real bugs. Handle the exception
types you actually know about and rethrow the rest.
{% /aside %}

## Fallbacks

Supplying a degraded result is legitimate when the caller can use one:

```csharp
public sealed class FallbackToCache : IQueryExceptionInterceptor<GetRates, Rates>
{
    private readonly IMemoryCache _cache;

    public FallbackToCache(IMemoryCache cache) => _cache = cache;

    public ValueTask<Rates?> HandleAsync(
        GetRates query, Rates? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (exception is HttpRequestException && _cache.TryGetValue<Rates>("rates", out var stale))
            return ValueTask.FromResult<Rates?>(stale);

        throw exception;
    }
}
```

## Ordering

Direct registrations first, then covariant ones, each by `[Weight]` descending — the same
rule as every other stage. The first interceptor that returns a value ends the stage;
interceptors after it do not run.

Put specific translations at a high weight and the blanket logger at a low one, so the logger
sees failures that the specific handlers declined.

## Aborts are not exceptions

`context.Abort()` does not raise anything. It ends the pipeline cleanly, so the exception
stage is not involved. `ExecutionAbortedException` exists for the runtime's own use; do not
catch it as a control-flow mechanism.

## Coverage

| Interface | `HandleAsync` |
|---|---|
| `ICommandExceptionInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object?>(ICommand, object?, Exception, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandExceptionInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object?>(TCommand, object?, Exception, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandExceptionInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult?>(TCommand, TResult?, Exception, IExecutionContext)` |
| `IQueryExceptionInterceptor` / `<TQuery, TResult>` | as above |
| `IEventExceptionInterceptor` / `<TEvent>` | `ValueTask<object?>(TEvent, ValueTask, Exception, IExecutionContext)` |

Next: [Final interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/final-interceptors).

A post-interceptor runs after the main handler and returns the result the caller will
receive. Like the pre stage, the return value is the point: a post-interceptor can observe
the result, replace it, or derive a new one.

```csharp
public sealed class AuditCreation : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    private readonly IAuditLog _audit;

    public AuditCreation(IAuditLog audit) => _audit = audit;

    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(
        CreateProduct command, Guid result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _audit.Record("product.created", result);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Post-interceptors run **only on success**. If the handler threw, or a pre-interceptor called
`Abort()`, the post stage is skipped entirely. Anything that must run regardless belongs in a
[final interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/final-interceptors).
{% /aside %}

## Shaping a result

The typed two-parameter form is the one to reach for when you are transforming:

```csharp
public sealed class MaskSecrets : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetUser, UserDto>
{
    public ValueTask<UserDto> HandleAsync(
        GetUser query, UserDto result, IExecutionContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(result with { Email = Mask(result.Email) });
}
```

Each post-interceptor in the stage sees the previous one's output, so transformations
compose in `[Weight]` order.

## Caching

Populate the cache after a successful query, and check it in the matching pre-interceptor:

```csharp
public sealed class CacheProduct : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IMemoryCache _cache;

    public CacheProduct(IMemoryCache cache) => _cache = cache;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct query, Product? result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (result is not null)
            _cache.Set(query.Id, result, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));

        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

The read side belongs in a pre-interceptor that calls `context.Abort(cached)` on a hit — that
skips the handler entirely.

## Blanket auditing

The non-generic form applies to every command in the module. It receives the result as
`object`, since there is no single type it could be:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordOutcome : ICommandPostInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Increment($"command.{command.GetType().Name}.ok");
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Because this matches covariantly, a message can opt out with `[ExcludeFromPipeline]` — see
[Polymorphism](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/polymorphism#opting-out).

## Events

An event has no result, so the event post-interceptor receives the `ValueTask` representing
the handler invocation rather than a value:

```csharp
public sealed class LogPublished : IEventPostInterceptor<OrderPlaced>
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        OrderPlaced @event, ValueTask result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Published {Event}", @event.Id);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(result);
    }
}
```

There is no two-parameter typed form for event post-interceptors, for the same reason.

## Coverage

| Interface | `HandleAsync` |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPostInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult>(TCommand, TResult, IExecutionContext)` |
| `IQueryPostInterceptor` / `<TQuery>` / `<TQuery, TResult>` | as above |
| `IEventPostInterceptor` / `<TEvent>` | `ValueTask<object>(TEvent, ValueTask, IExecutionContext)` |

Next: [Exception interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors).

A pre-interceptor runs before the main handler and returns the message the rest of the
pipeline will see. That return value is the whole design: a pre-interceptor can pass the
message through unchanged, replace it, or abort the pipeline outright.

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    private readonly ILogger<LogCommands> _logger;

    public LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Registered automatically by [compile-time discovery](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/compile-time-discovery) —
there is nothing to add at startup.

## Validation

Validation is the canonical pre-interceptor: it belongs before the handler, it applies to
many messages, and it has nothing to do with the operation itself.

```csharp
public sealed class ValidateCommands<TCommand> : ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>
    where TCommand : ICommand
{
    private readonly IEnumerable<IValidator<TCommand>> _validators;

    public ValidateCommands(IEnumerable<IValidator<TCommand>> validators)
        => _validators = validators;

    public async ValueTask<TCommand> HandleAsync(TCommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        foreach (var validator in _validators)
        {
            var result = await validator.ValidateAsync(command, context.CancellationToken);
            if (!result.IsValid)
                throw new ValidationException(result.Errors);
        }

        return command;
    }
}
```

Throwing propagates to the [exception interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors),
which is usually where you want a validation failure translated into a response shape.

{% aside type="note" %}
An open generic interceptor like `ValidateCommands<TCommand>` cannot be closed by the source
generator, so it registers through the runtime fallback. Register it explicitly if you need
it in a trimmed or AOT build.
{% /aside %}

## Authorization and short-circuiting

When the pipeline should stop without an exception, call `Abort`:

```csharp
public sealed class RequirePermission : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct>
{
    private readonly ICurrentUser _user;

    public RequirePermission(ICurrentUser user) => _user = user;

    public ValueTask<DeleteProduct> HandleAsync(DeleteProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.Can("products:delete"))
            context.Abort();

        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

If the command produces a result, `Abort` needs one — nothing downstream will produce it:

```csharp
context.Abort(Guid.Empty);   // CreateProduct : ICommand<Guid>
```

An abort skips the remaining pre-interceptors, the handler and the post stage. **Final
interceptors still run**, which is what makes them safe for cleanup.

## Enriching the context

Anything a pre-interceptor puts in the context is visible to the handler and to every later
stage of the same dispatch:

```csharp
public sealed class ResolveTenant : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        context.Set("tenant", _tenants.Current);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Read it back with `context.Get<Tenant>("tenant")`, or `TryGet` when absence is possible.

## Rewriting the message

A pre-interceptor has no result to carry, so the single-parameter form returns the command
type directly — rewriting is just returning a new instance:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

The handler receives the trimmed record. So does every pre-interceptor after this one — the
stage is a chain, each link seeing the previous link's output.

Reach for the two-parameter form only when you return a *different*, derived command type;
`TModified` must derive from `TCommand`.

## Ordering

Within the pre stage: direct registrations first, then covariant ones, each ordered by
`[Weight]` descending.

```csharp
[Weight(200)] public sealed class Authorize  : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Validate   : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
[Weight(10)]  public sealed class LogCommand : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
```

Authorize, then validate, then log — which is the order you want, since there is no reason to
validate a request the caller is not allowed to make.

See [Groups and weights](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights) for the
full ordering rules and for restricting interceptors to a subset of dispatches.

## Coverage

| Interface | `HandleAsync` |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<TCommand>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` | `ValueTask<TModified>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` |

The non-generic form intercepts any command, so it returns `object`. The single-parameter
form knows the command type and returns it — a pre-interceptor carries no result, so the
message is the only thing there is to return. The two-parameter form returns a different,
derived type.

Queries and events offer the same three shapes as `IQueryPreInterceptor…` and
`IEventPreInterceptor…`.

Next: [Post-interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/cross-cutting-concerns/post-interceptors).

This walks through a minimal but complete setup: one command, one query, one event, and the
startup code that registers all of them without a single hand-written registration call.

## Step 1 — Install

```bash
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare.SourceGenerator
```

## Step 2 — Declare a command and its handler

A command is a message that *does* something. It may or may not produce a result; use
`ICommand<TResult>` when it does.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name, decimal Price) : ICommand<Guid>;

public sealed class CreateProductHandler : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public CreateProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => _store.AddAsync(command.Name, command.Price, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Two things worth noticing:

- The handler returns **`ValueTask<TResult>`**. A handler that completes synchronously
  allocates nothing.
- The cancellation token arrives on the **context**, not as a separate parameter. There is
  no way to forget to thread it through.

## Step 3 — Declare a query and its handler

A query *asks* something and always produces a result.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;

public sealed record GetProduct(Guid Id) : IQuery<Product?>;

public sealed class GetProductHandler : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public GetProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, IExecutionContext context)
        => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Step 4 — Declare an event and its handlers

An event is broadcast: every matching handler runs, and there may be none.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;

public sealed record ProductCreated(Guid Id, string Name) : IEvent;

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _mailer.SendAsync(@event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}

public sealed class UpdateSearchIndex : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _index.AddAsync(@event.Id, @event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Step 5 — Register everything

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Generated;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddQueryModule(q => q.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(e => e.RegisterGenerated()));
```

That is the whole registration. `RegisterGenerated()` does not exist in any NuGet package —
the source generator emits it into *your* compilation, already containing every handler it
found. Add a handler anywhere in the project and the next build picks it up.

{% aside type="tip" %}
`using Stella.Ergosfare.Generated;` is required — that is the namespace the generator emits
into. A missing `RegisterGenerated()` is almost always a missing using.
{% /aside %}

## Step 6 — Dispatch

Inject the mediator interface for the module you need. They are separate on purpose: a
component that only reads does not get the ability to send commands.

```csharp
app.MapPost("/products", async (
    CreateProduct command,
    ICommandMediator commands,
    IEventMediator events) =>
{
    var id = await commands.SendAsync(command);
    await events.PublishAsync(new ProductCreated(id, command.Name));
    return Results.Created($"/products/{id}", id);
});

app.MapGet("/products/{id:guid}", async (Guid id, IQueryMediator queries) =>
{
    var product = await queries.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
    return product is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(product);
});
```

| Module | Interface | Call |
|---|---|---|
| Commands | `ICommandMediator` | `SendAsync(command)` |
| Queries | `IQueryMediator` | `QueryAsync(query)`, `StreamAsync(streamQuery)` |
| Events | `IEventMediator` | `PublishAsync(@event)` |

## Step 7 — Add a cross-cutting concern

Interceptors attach to the pipeline without touching handlers. A pre-interceptor registered
against a base type or interface applies to every message assignable to it:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions.Attributes;

[Weight(100)]                                  // higher weight runs earlier
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    private readonly ILogger<LogCommands> _logger;

    public LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);   // pass the message through
    }
}
```

No registration needed — the generator finds it exactly like a handler.

{% aside type="note" %}
A pre-interceptor returns the message rather than `void`, because it is allowed to *rewrite*
it: whatever you return is what the next stage sees. Returning the message unchanged, as
above, is the pass-through case. See
[Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/interceptors).
{% /aside %}

## Where to go next

- [Compile-time discovery](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/compile-time-discovery) — what the generator sees, and how to control it.
- [Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/interceptors) — all four stages and how they compose.
- [Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/execution-context) — pooling, lifetime, and the rules that come with it.
- [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/core-concepts/nested-dispatch) — calling the mediator from inside a handler.

An event handler implements `IEventHandler<TEvent>`. There is no result to return:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    private readonly IMailer _mailer;

    public SendWelcomeEmail(IMailer mailer) => _mailer = mailer;

    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _mailer.SendAsync(@event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Any number of handlers can exist for one event — that is the point of the module. Adding a
second one is not a configuration error, unlike with commands and queries.

## Handlers must be independent

Handlers for the same event run in a defined order (direct before covariant, then `[Weight]`
descending), but you should not build on that order. Two rules follow:

- **Do not pass data between handlers.** If handler B needs what handler A produced, they are
  one operation and belong in one handler — or A should be a command that publishes the event
  B reacts to.
- **Do not assume the others succeeded.** A handler that must run only after another
  succeeded is describing a workflow, not a broadcast.

The item bag on the context does let handlers share data within one dispatch, which makes
this easy to violate. Reserve it for
[interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/interceptors) passing data to the pipeline, not
for handler-to-handler communication.

## When a handler throws

The exception propagates out of `PublishAsync`, and the
[exception stage](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors) runs. That
means one failing subscriber can fail the whole publish — and, if the publish is inside a
command handler, the command with it.

When a subscriber's failure genuinely should not fail the publisher, contain it in the
handler:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
{
    try
    {
        await _searchIndex.AddAsync(@event.Id, @event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
    }
    catch (Exception ex) when (ex is not OperationCanceledException)
    {
        _logger.LogError(ex, "Indexing {Product} failed; continuing", @event.Id);
    }
}
```

Letting cancellation through is deliberate — a cancelled dispatch should stay cancelled.

For work that must survive process failure, do not do it in the handler at all: write to an
outbox in the handler and let a separate worker do the delivery.

## Covariant handlers

A handler registered against a base type or interface receives every assignable event:

```csharp
public sealed class AppendToOutbox : IEventHandler<IDomainEvent>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(IDomainEvent @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _outbox.AppendAsync(@event, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Handlers registered for the concrete event type run first, then covariant ones. This is what
lets an outbox, an audit sink or a metrics collector subscribe to a whole family of events
with a single class.

## Lifetimes

Event handlers resolve from the calling DI scope on each dispatch, like every other handler.
A handler holding a `DbContext` should be **scoped**. See
[Configuring Ergosfare](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/configuring#handler-lifetimes).

Because a publish may fan out to many handlers, this is the module where transient handlers
allocate most visibly — one instance per handler per dispatch. Register them as singletons
when they are stateless.

## Publishing from a handler

Use a [nested scope](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch) so the inner dispatch gets
a clean item bag and the parent's cancellation token:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(OrderPlaced @event, IExecutionContext context)
{
    using var scope = context.CreateScope();
    await _events.PublishAsync(new InvoiceRequested(@event.OrderId), scope.Context);
}
```

Next: [Modules and extensions](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-components/modules-extensions).

A query handler implements `IQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>`:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed class GetProductHandler : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public GetProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, IExecutionContext context)
        => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

As everywhere else: `ValueTask` return, cancellation on the context, dependencies through the
constructor, and no registration call — the source generator finds it.

## One handler per query

Like commands, a query has exactly one main handler. Two registrations for the same query
type raise `MultipleHandlerFoundException` at dispatch.

## Streaming

`IStreamQueryHandler<TQuery, TResult>` returns `IAsyncEnumerable<TResult>` instead:

Note the method name: streaming handlers implement **`StreamAsync`**, not `HandleAsync`, and
there is no separate cancellation-token parameter.

```csharp
public sealed class StreamProductsHandler : IStreamQueryHandler<StreamProducts, Product>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public StreamProductsHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public async IAsyncEnumerable<Product> StreamAsync(
        StreamProducts query, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        // Read what you need off the context before the first yield: from here on the
        // method body runs during the consumer's enumeration, not during the dispatch call.
        var cancellationToken = context.CancellationToken;

        await foreach (var page in _store.PagesAsync(query.PageSize, cancellationToken))
            foreach (var product in page)
                yield return product;
    }
}
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
An iterator method body does not start running until the consumer enumerates it. Capture
anything you need from `IExecutionContext` into locals before the first `yield return` rather
than touching `context` inside the loop — see
[Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/execution-context#pooling-and-the-lifetime-rule).
{% /aside %}

Consume it with `await foreach`:

```csharp
await foreach (var product in _queries.StreamAsync(new StreamProducts(100), ct))
    await WriteAsync(product);
```

## Returning "not found"

If the query is declared `IQuery<Product?>`, returning `null` is the contract and callers must
handle it. If it is declared `IQuery<Product>`, throw — and let an
[exception interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors)
translate that into whatever the edge of your application returns:

```csharp
public ValueTask<Product> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, IExecutionContext context)
    => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken)
        ?? throw new KeyNotFoundException($"Product {query.Id} not found");
```

Pick one and be consistent across the codebase; mixing the two is what makes callers write
both a null check and a try/catch.

## Caching

The pipeline is a better place for caching than the handler, because it keeps the handler
about the data:

- A [pre-interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/pre-interceptors) checks the
  cache and calls `context.Abort(cached)` on a hit, skipping the handler entirely.
- A [post-interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/post-interceptors) populates
  the cache on the way out.

Scope both to a marker interface such as `ICacheableQuery` so only the queries you opted in
pay for it.

## Lifetimes

Handlers resolve from the calling DI scope on every dispatch. A query handler that holds a
`DbContext` should be **scoped**, matching the context's own lifetime — a singleton handler
capturing a scoped dependency is the usual source of "cannot access a disposed object" at
runtime. See
[Configuring Ergosfare](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/configuring#handler-lifetimes).

Next: [Defining an event](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-components/event/event-definition).

`IExecutionContext` travels with a dispatch. It carries the cancellation token, a key/value
bag for that dispatch, and the controls that let a handler or interceptor stop the pipeline.

Two properties define everything else about it:

1. **It is a parameter, never ambient.** Every handler and interceptor receives it
   explicitly. There is no `AsyncLocal`, no thread-local, and no static accessor.
2. **It is pooled.** A dispatch rents a context and returns it on completion. The context is
   valid **only for the duration of that dispatch**.

{% aside type="danger" %}
`AmbientExecutionContext` was **removed in v2**. If you are porting v1 code that reached for
it, see [Migrating from v1](/ergosfare.docs/preview/migration/v1-to-v2). The context parameter is now
the only access path.
{% /aside %}

## What it carries

```csharp
public interface IExecutionContext
{
    CancellationToken CancellationToken { get; }
    IDictionary<object, object?> Items { get; }

    void Set(string key, object item);
    bool Has(string key);
    T Get<T>(string key) where T : notnull;
    bool TryGet<T>(string key, out T item);

    void Abort(object? messageResult = null);
    ExecutionContextScope CreateScope();
}
```

### Cancellation

```csharp
public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
    => _store.AddAsync(command.Name, command.Price, context.CancellationToken);
```

The token comes from the mediator call — either the `CancellationToken` argument or, for the
context-taking overloads, inherited from the parent context. Because it arrives on the
context rather than as a separate handler parameter, forgetting to thread it is not possible.

### The item bag

`Set`/`Get`/`Has`/`TryGet` share data along one dispatch. A pre-interceptor can stash
something the handler and post-interceptors read back:

```csharp
public sealed class StartTimer : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        context.Set("started", Stopwatch.GetTimestamp());
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}

public sealed class StopTimer : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ICommand command, object? result, Exception? ex, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("started", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started));

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

`Get<T>` throws `KeyNotFoundException` if the key is absent and `InvalidCastException` if the
stored value is the wrong type. Use `TryGet<T>` when absence is expected.

You can seed the bag for a dispatch from the mediation settings:

```csharp
var settings = new CommandMediationSettings { Items = { ["tenant"] = tenantId } };
await commands.SendAsync(command, settings);
```

### Aborting

`Abort()` ends the pipeline immediately — no further handlers or interceptors run. If the
message has a result type and you abort before the main handler, you must supply the result:

```csharp
public sealed class RejectUnauthorized : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.CanCreateProducts)
            context.Abort(Guid.Empty);   // result required: the pipeline still owes one

        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

`CreateProduct` is an `ICommand<Guid>`, so the pipeline owes the caller a `Guid`. Aborting
before the main handler runs means nothing else will produce one — hence the argument. For a
command with no result, `Abort()` takes nothing.

## Pooling and the lifetime rule

Contexts are rented from a `[ThreadStatic]`-first pool — a plain load and store on the common
same-thread cycle — and returned when the dispatch completes. There is a synchronous fast
path that skips the async state machine entirely when the whole pipeline completes
synchronously.

This is where the ~2.3 MB per 100 000 dispatches comes from. It also imposes one rule:

{% aside type="danger" %}
**Never hold onto a context after its dispatch returns.** Once the dispatch completes the
context goes back to the pool and will be handed to an unrelated dispatch. Capturing it in a
field, a closure that outlives the call, or a fire-and-forget task will read another
message's state.
{% /aside %}

```csharp
// Wrong — the context is recycled the moment HandleAsync returns.
public ValueTask HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
{
    _ = Task.Run(async () =>
    {
        await Task.Delay(1000);
        context.Set("late", true);      // this context now belongs to someone else
    });
    return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
}
```

If you need something after the dispatch, copy the values out before returning.

## No service provider, on purpose

`IExecutionContext` exposes no `IServiceProvider`. Handler resolution belongs to the
dispatcher, which resolves instances from the calling DI scope. If a handler needs a service,
take it in the constructor — that keeps the dependency visible to the container and to
whoever reads the class.

## Nested dispatch

`CreateScope()` opens a child context for calling the mediator from inside a handler. It gets
its own clean item bag and inherits the parent's cancellation token. That has enough detail
to deserve its own page: [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

Next: [Nested dispatch](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/nested-dispatch).

Interceptors are how cross-cutting concerns attach to a dispatch without handlers knowing
about them. They are discovered and registered exactly like handlers, and they run as part of
the same pre-built pipeline plan.

There are **four stages**:

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

- **pre** runs before the handler and may **rewrite the message**.
- **post** runs after a successful handler and may **rewrite the result**.
- **exception** runs when the handler or an interceptor threw, and may **supply a result**.
- **final** always runs last, on success and on failure alike.

Within a stage, order is `[Weight]`-descending, direct registrations before covariant ones.
See [Groups and weights](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights).

## Pre-interceptors

A pre-interceptor returns the message that the next stage will see. Returning the input
unchanged is the pass-through case:

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Three arities are available, trading breadth for type safety:

| Interface | `HandleAsync` signature | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, IExecutionContext)` | Applies to every command |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` | Applies to one command type or hierarchy |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` | `ValueTask<TModified>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` | You actually rewrite the message and want it typed |

In the two-parameter form, `TModified` must derive from `TCommand`. It exists for rewriting,
so if you are only observing, use the one-parameter form:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct, CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

## Post-interceptors

A post-interceptor receives the result and returns the result the caller will get:

```csharp
public sealed class StampProduct : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, Guid result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _audit.Record(command.Name, result);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

| Interface | `HandleAsync` signature |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPostInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult>(TCommand, TResult, IExecutionContext)` |

Post-interceptors do **not** run when the pipeline threw. That is what the exception stage is
for.

## Exception interceptors

```csharp
public sealed class SwallowNotFound : ICommandExceptionInterceptor<GetOrCreate, Guid>
{
    public ValueTask<Guid?> HandleAsync(
        GetOrCreate command, Guid? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (exception is not KeyNotFoundException)
            throw exception;                 // not ours — let it propagate

        return ValueTask.FromResult<Guid?>(Guid.Empty);
    }
}
```

The interceptor receives whatever result had been produced so far (often `null`) and the
exception. Returning a value satisfies the caller; rethrowing lets the exception propagate.

## Final interceptors

Final interceptors always run — success, failure, or abort — which makes them the right place
for cleanup, metrics and audit trails. They cannot change anything:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordDuration : ICommandFinalInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object? result, Exception? exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (context.TryGet<long>("started", out var started))
            _metrics.Record(command.GetType().Name, Stopwatch.GetElapsedTime(started), exception);

        return ValueTask.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

Note the signature: both `result` and `exception` are nullable, because exactly one of them
is meaningful on any given run.

## Coverage across modules

Every module offers all four stages, but not every arity exists everywhere — events have no
typed result, so their post and exception interceptors receive a `ValueTask` rather than a
result value.

| Stage | Commands | Queries | Events |
|---|---|---|---|
| Pre | `ICommandPreInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TModified>` | `IQueryPreInterceptor` `<TQuery>` `<TQuery, TModified>` | `IEventPreInterceptor` `<TEvent>` `<TEvent, TModified>` |
| Post | `ICommandPostInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TResult>` | `IQueryPostInterceptor` `<TQuery>` `<TQuery, TResult>` | `IEventPostInterceptor` `<TEvent>` |
| Exception | `ICommandExceptionInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TResult>` | `IQueryExceptionInterceptor` `<TQuery, TResult>` | `IEventExceptionInterceptor` `<TEvent>` |
| Final | `ICommandFinalInterceptor` `<TCommand>` `<TCommand, TResult>` | `IQueryFinalInterceptor` `<TQuery, TResult>` | `IEventFinalInterceptor` `<TEvent>` |

The [API reference](/ergosfare.docs/preview/api) carries the exact signature for each one.

## Covariant matching

An interceptor registered against a base type or interface applies to **every assignable
message**. This is the main reason to prefer a marker interface over a base class:

```csharp
public interface IAuditedCommand : ICommand;

public sealed class AuditTrail : ICommandPreInterceptor<IAuditedCommand>
{
    public ValueTask<IAuditedCommand> HandleAsync(IAuditedCommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _audit.Log(command);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

Every command implementing `IAuditedCommand` now goes through `AuditTrail`, with no
registration per command.

Within a stage, interceptors registered for the message type itself run **before** covariantly
matched ones. See [Polymorphism](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/polymorphism).

## Opting a message out

`[ExcludeFromPipeline]` on a *message* removes it from covariantly matched interceptors —
useful for a high-volume message you do not want the blanket logging interceptor to touch:

```csharp
[ExcludeFromPipeline]
public sealed record Heartbeat : ICommand;

[ExcludeFromPipeline("logging")]     // or just one group
public sealed record Tick : ICommand;
```

Interceptors registered for the message type *itself* always run — they were written for that
message deliberately. Main handlers are never affected.

## What interceptors are not

They are not handlers. An interceptor does not perform the operation; it observes, enriches,
short-circuits or cleans up around it. If a piece of logic is the point of the message, it
belongs in the handler.

Next: [Polymorphism](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/polymorphism).

A handler often needs to mediate further messages: reserve stock while placing an order,
publish an event after a command succeeds. Ergosfare handles this with an explicit child
scope rather than ambient state.

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(PlaceOrder msg, IExecutionContext ctx)
{
    using var scope = ctx.CreateScope();   // struct scope — allocates nothing

    var reserved = await _commands.SendAsync(new ReserveStock(msg.OrderId), scope.Context);
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(msg.OrderId), scope.Context);
}
```

## What the child gets

**Clean items.** The child starts with an empty item bag. Anything an inner interceptor
stashes stays inner — it cannot leak into the outer dispatch and confuse an outer
post-interceptor.

**The parent's cancellation token.** Inherited, not re-derived. Nested work cannot escape the
outer cancellation chain, and there is no token parameter to forget.

**Its own pipeline.** The inner message runs its own plan: its own interceptors, its own
handlers, its own abort semantics.

## Passing the child

Every mediator facade has an overload taking an `IExecutionContext`:

```csharp
ValueTask        SendAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context, CommandMediationSettings? settings = null);
ValueTask<T>     SendAsync<T>(ICommand<T> command, IExecutionContext context, CommandMediationSettings? settings = null);
ValueTask<T>     QueryAsync<T>(IQuery<T> query, IExecutionContext context, QueryMediationSettings? settings = null);
ValueTask        PublishAsync(IEvent @event, IExecutionContext context, EventMediationSettings? settings = null);
```

Pass `scope.Context`, not `ctx`. Passing the parent would run the inner dispatch on the
outer context and defeat the isolation.

## Abort is scoped too

An `Abort()` inside the child ends **only the inner pipeline**. The outer handler resumes at
the line after the inner call and decides what happens next:

```csharp
public async ValueTask<bool> HandleAsync(PlaceOrder msg, IExecutionContext ctx)
{
    using var reserve = ctx.CreateScope();
    var stock = await _commands.SendAsync(new ReserveStock(msg.OrderId), reserve.Context);

    if (!stock.Reserved)
        return false;      // inner abort did not touch this pipeline

    using var notify = ctx.CreateScope();
    await _events.PublishAsync(new OrderPlaced(msg.OrderId), notify.Context);
    return true;
}
```

This is the part ambient-state mediators cannot offer safely. With an `AsyncLocal` context,
an inner dispatch either overwrites the ambient value — requiring a restore step that a
`finally` block has to get exactly right — or shares state with the outer pipeline. Here
there is nothing ambient to overwrite, so there is nothing to restore.

## Parallel inner dispatches

Separate scopes are independent, so fanning out is safe:

```csharp
public async ValueTask HandleAsync(RebuildAll msg, IExecutionContext ctx)
{
    await Task.WhenAll(msg.TenantIds.Select(async id =>
    {
        using var scope = ctx.CreateScope();
        await _commands.SendAsync(new RebuildTenant(id), scope.Context);
    }));
}
```

Each iteration rents its own child context. They share the parent's cancellation token and
nothing else.

{% aside type="caution" %}
Do not share one scope across concurrent inner dispatches. One scope means one context, and
a context is single-dispatch state.
{% /aside %}

## Lifetime

`ExecutionContextScope` is a `readonly struct` implementing `IDisposable`, and `using`
returns the pooled child. As with any pooled context, the child **must not be used after the
scope is disposed** — see
[Execution context](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/execution-context#pooling-and-the-lifetime-rule).

```csharp
// Wrong — the child is back in the pool by the time this runs.
IExecutionContext leaked;
using (var scope = ctx.CreateScope())
    leaked = scope.Context;

leaked.Set("oops", true);
```

Next: [Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/interceptors).

Ergosfare's core knows about messages, handlers, interceptors and pipeline plans. It knows
nothing about *commands*, *queries* or *events*. Those three are **modules** built on the same
extension point you can use yourself.

That is not a metaphor. `AddCommandModule` is four lines:

```csharp
public static IModuleRegistry AddCommandModule(
    this IModuleRegistry moduleRegistry,
    Action<CommandModuleBuilder> builderAction)
{
    moduleRegistry.Register(new CommandModule(builderAction));
    return moduleRegistry;
}
```

## The contract

A module is anything implementing `IModule`:

```csharp
public interface IModule
{
    void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration);
}
```

`Build` runs once, at registration time. It receives everything a module needs:

```csharp
public interface IModuleConfiguration
{
    IServiceCollection Services { get; }        // register your own services
    IMessageRegistry MessageRegistry { get; }   // register messages, handlers, interceptors
}
```

Here is the whole command module:

```csharp
internal class CommandModule : IModule
{
    private readonly Action<CommandModuleBuilder> _builder;

    public CommandModule(Action<CommandModuleBuilder> builder) => _builder = builder;

    public void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _builder(new CommandModuleBuilder(configuration.MessageRegistry));

        configuration.Services.TryAddTransient<ICommandMediator, CommandMediator>();
    }
}
```

Two responsibilities: run the user's registration lambda against a builder, and add the
module's own facade to DI. `TryAdd` rather than `Add`, so registering a module twice is
harmless.

**Transient, not scoped.** A mediator facade is stateless — the only thing it captures is the
provider that resolved it, and a transient service is handed the resolving scope's provider
just as a scoped one is, so handlers still resolve from the calling scope. Registering it
scoped would take the scope's lock and write the instance into the scope's resolved-services
dictionary on every dispatch that creates its own scope, with nothing to amortize it. Follow
this in your own module. See [Performance](/ergosfare.docs/preview/advanced/performance#mediator-lifetime).

## Writing your own module

The case for a custom module is a *new message shape* — something that is not a command, a
query or an event, but wants the same pipeline, the same interceptor stages and the same
compile-time discovery.

```csharp
public sealed class SagaModule : IModule
{
    private readonly Action<SagaModuleBuilder> _builder;

    public SagaModule(Action<SagaModuleBuilder> builder) => _builder = builder;

    public void Build(IModuleConfiguration configuration)
    {
        _builder(new SagaModuleBuilder(configuration.MessageRegistry));

        configuration.Services.TryAddTransient<ISagaMediator, SagaMediator>();
    }
}

public static class SagaModuleRegistryExtensions
{
    public static IModuleRegistry AddSagaModule(
        this IModuleRegistry registry, Action<SagaModuleBuilder> builderAction)
    {
        registry.Register(new SagaModule(builderAction));
        return registry;
    }
}
```

Used exactly like the built-in modules:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddSagaModule(s => s.RegisterFromAssembly(typeof(MySaga).Assembly)));
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
The source generator only emits `RegisterGenerated()` for the three built-in module builders.
A custom module has to feed the registry itself — through explicit `Register<T>()` calls, its
own `RegisterFromAssembly`, or `RegisterDescriptors(...)` if you pre-compute descriptors
yourself.
{% /aside %}

## Module builders

Every builder implements the same three entry points:

```csharp
public interface IModuleBuilder
{
    IModuleBuilder Register<T>();
    IModuleBuilder Register(Type type);
    IModuleBuilder RegisterFromAssembly(Assembly assembly);
}
```

The concrete builders add module-specific overloads on top — `CommandModuleBuilder` returns
`CommandModuleBuilder` from each call so chains stay typed, and adds
`RegisterDescriptors(IEnumerable<IHandlerDescriptor>)`, which is what generated registration
uses to hand over pre-computed descriptors without reflection.

## Only take what you need

Because the modules are independent, an application that never publishes events never
registers the event module, never resolves `IEventMediator`, and never carries its assembly:

```csharp
services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated()));   // that's it
```

The same applies at the package level — see
[Installation](/ergosfare.docs/preview/getting-started/installation).

## The core module

`AddCoreModule` registers messages, handlers and interceptors that are not tied to any of the
three built-in shapes. It is rarely needed directly; the built-in modules already configure
what the runtime requires.

Next: [Modules and extensions](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-components/modules-extensions) walks
through the shipped modules in detail.

An exception interceptor runs when the handler — or anything earlier in the pipeline — threw.
It receives the message, whatever result had been produced so far, and the exception, and it
decides what the caller sees.

```csharp
public sealed class TranslateNotFound : IQueryExceptionInterceptor<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct query, Product? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (exception is KeyNotFoundException)
            return ValueTask.FromResult<Product?>(null);

        throw exception;      // not ours — let it propagate
    }
}
```

Two outcomes, and nothing in between:

- **Return a value** — the exception is considered handled, and the caller receives that value.
- **Throw** — the exception propagates. Rethrow the original when it is not yours to handle.

## Where it sits

```
pre*  →  main handler  →  post*        →  final*
                       ↘  exception*  ↗
```

Post-interceptors do not run when the pipeline threw; the exception stage takes their place.
Final interceptors run either way.

## Logging without swallowing

The common case is enriching the failure and letting it continue:

```csharp
public sealed class LogFailures : ICommandExceptionInterceptor
{
    private readonly ILogger<LogFailures> _logger;

    public LogFailures(ILogger<LogFailures> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public ValueTask<object?> HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogError(exception, "{Command} failed", command.GetType().Name);
        throw exception;
    }
}
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Returning a value from an exception interceptor **swallows the exception**. A blanket
interceptor that returns `null` on every failure will hide real bugs. Handle the exception
types you actually know about and rethrow the rest.
{% /aside %}

## Fallbacks

Supplying a degraded result is legitimate when the caller can use one:

```csharp
public sealed class FallbackToCache : IQueryExceptionInterceptor<GetRates, Rates>
{
    private readonly IMemoryCache _cache;

    public FallbackToCache(IMemoryCache cache) => _cache = cache;

    public ValueTask<Rates?> HandleAsync(
        GetRates query, Rates? result, Exception exception, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (exception is HttpRequestException && _cache.TryGetValue<Rates>("rates", out var stale))
            return ValueTask.FromResult<Rates?>(stale);

        throw exception;
    }
}
```

## Ordering

Direct registrations first, then covariant ones, each by `[Weight]` descending — the same
rule as every other stage. The first interceptor that returns a value ends the stage;
interceptors after it do not run.

Put specific translations at a high weight and the blanket logger at a low one, so the logger
sees failures that the specific handlers declined.

## Aborts are not exceptions

`context.Abort()` does not raise anything. It ends the pipeline cleanly, so the exception
stage is not involved. `ExecutionAbortedException` exists for the runtime's own use; do not
catch it as a control-flow mechanism.

## Coverage

| Interface | `HandleAsync` |
|---|---|
| `ICommandExceptionInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object?>(ICommand, object?, Exception, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandExceptionInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object?>(TCommand, object?, Exception, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandExceptionInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult?>(TCommand, TResult?, Exception, IExecutionContext)` |
| `IQueryExceptionInterceptor` / `<TQuery, TResult>` | as above |
| `IEventExceptionInterceptor` / `<TEvent>` | `ValueTask<object?>(TEvent, ValueTask, Exception, IExecutionContext)` |

Next: [Final interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/final-interceptors).

A post-interceptor runs after the main handler and returns the result the caller will
receive. Like the pre stage, the return value is the point: a post-interceptor can observe
the result, replace it, or derive a new one.

```csharp
public sealed class AuditCreation : ICommandPostInterceptor<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    private readonly IAuditLog _audit;

    public AuditCreation(IAuditLog audit) => _audit = audit;

    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(
        CreateProduct command, Guid result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _audit.Record("product.created", result);
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

{% aside type="caution" %}
Post-interceptors run **only on success**. If the handler threw, or a pre-interceptor called
`Abort()`, the post stage is skipped entirely. Anything that must run regardless belongs in a
[final interceptor](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/final-interceptors).
{% /aside %}

## Shaping a result

The typed two-parameter form is the one to reach for when you are transforming:

```csharp
public sealed class MaskSecrets : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetUser, UserDto>
{
    public ValueTask<UserDto> HandleAsync(
        GetUser query, UserDto result, IExecutionContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(result with { Email = Mask(result.Email) });
}
```

Each post-interceptor in the stage sees the previous one's output, so transformations
compose in `[Weight]` order.

## Caching

Populate the cache after a successful query, and check it in the matching pre-interceptor:

```csharp
public sealed class CacheProduct : IQueryPostInterceptor<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IMemoryCache _cache;

    public CacheProduct(IMemoryCache cache) => _cache = cache;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(
        GetProduct query, Product? result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (result is not null)
            _cache.Set(query.Id, result, TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5));

        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

The read side belongs in a pre-interceptor that calls `context.Abort(cached)` on a hit — that
skips the handler entirely.

## Blanket auditing

The non-generic form applies to every command in the module. It receives the result as
`object`, since there is no single type it could be:

```csharp
public sealed class RecordOutcome : ICommandPostInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        ICommand command, object result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _metrics.Increment($"command.{command.GetType().Name}.ok");
        return ValueTask.FromResult(result);
    }
}
```

Because this matches covariantly, a message can opt out with `[ExcludeFromPipeline]` — see
[Polymorphism](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/polymorphism#opting-out).

## Events

An event has no result, so the event post-interceptor receives the `ValueTask` representing
the handler invocation rather than a value:

```csharp
public sealed class LogPublished : IEventPostInterceptor<OrderPlaced>
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(
        OrderPlaced @event, ValueTask result, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Published {Event}", @event.Id);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(result);
    }
}
```

There is no two-parameter typed form for event post-interceptors, for the same reason.

## Coverage

| Interface | `HandleAsync` |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPostInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<object>(TCommand, object, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPostInterceptor<TCommand, TResult>` | `ValueTask<TResult>(TCommand, TResult, IExecutionContext)` |
| `IQueryPostInterceptor` / `<TQuery>` / `<TQuery, TResult>` | as above |
| `IEventPostInterceptor` / `<TEvent>` | `ValueTask<object>(TEvent, ValueTask, IExecutionContext)` |

Next: [Exception interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors).

A pre-interceptor runs before the main handler and returns the message the rest of the
pipeline will see. That return value is the whole design: a pre-interceptor can pass the
message through unchanged, replace it, or abort the pipeline outright.

```csharp
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    private readonly ILogger<LogCommands> _logger;

    public LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Registered automatically by [compile-time discovery](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/compile-time-discovery) —
there is nothing to add at startup.

## Validation

Validation is the canonical pre-interceptor: it belongs before the handler, it applies to
many messages, and it has nothing to do with the operation itself.

```csharp
public sealed class ValidateCommands<TCommand> : ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>
    where TCommand : ICommand
{
    private readonly IEnumerable<IValidator<TCommand>> _validators;

    public ValidateCommands(IEnumerable<IValidator<TCommand>> validators)
        => _validators = validators;

    public async ValueTask<TCommand> HandleAsync(TCommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        foreach (var validator in _validators)
        {
            var result = await validator.ValidateAsync(command, context.CancellationToken);
            if (!result.IsValid)
                throw new ValidationException(result.Errors);
        }

        return command;
    }
}
```

Throwing propagates to the [exception interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/exception-interceptors),
which is usually where you want a validation failure translated into a response shape.

{% aside type="note" %}
An open generic interceptor like `ValidateCommands<TCommand>` cannot be closed by the source
generator, so it registers through the runtime fallback. Register it explicitly if you need
it in a trimmed or AOT build.
{% /aside %}

## Authorization and short-circuiting

When the pipeline should stop without an exception, call `Abort`:

```csharp
public sealed class RequirePermission : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct>
{
    private readonly ICurrentUser _user;

    public RequirePermission(ICurrentUser user) => _user = user;

    public ValueTask<DeleteProduct> HandleAsync(DeleteProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        if (!_user.Can("products:delete"))
            context.Abort();

        return ValueTask.FromResult(command);
    }
}
```

If the command produces a result, `Abort` needs one — nothing downstream will produce it:

```csharp
context.Abort(Guid.Empty);   // CreateProduct : ICommand<Guid>
```

An abort skips the remaining pre-interceptors, the handler and the post stage. **Final
interceptors still run**, which is what makes them safe for cleanup.

## Enriching the context

Anything a pre-interceptor puts in the context is visible to the handler and to every later
stage of the same dispatch:

```csharp
public sealed class ResolveTenant : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        context.Set("tenant", _tenants.Current);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);
    }
}
```

Read it back with `context.Get<Tenant>("tenant")`, or `TryGet` when absence is possible.

## Rewriting the message

A pre-interceptor has no result to carry, so the single-parameter form returns the command
type directly — rewriting is just returning a new instance:

```csharp
public sealed class NormalizeName : ICommandPreInterceptor<CreateProduct>
{
    public ValueTask<CreateProduct> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => ValueTask.FromResult(command with { Name = command.Name.Trim() });
}
```

The handler receives the trimmed record. So does every pre-interceptor after this one — the
stage is a chain, each link seeing the previous link's output.

Reach for the two-parameter form only when you return a *different*, derived command type;
`TModified` must derive from `TCommand`.

## Ordering

Within the pre stage: direct registrations first, then covariant ones, each ordered by
`[Weight]` descending.

```csharp
[Weight(200)] public sealed class Authorize  : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
[Weight(100)] public sealed class Validate   : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
[Weight(10)]  public sealed class LogCommand : ICommandPreInterceptor<DeleteProduct> { … }
```

Authorize, then validate, then log — which is the order you want, since there is no reason to
validate a request the caller is not allowed to make.

See [Groups and weights](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/groups-and-weights) for the
full ordering rules and for restricting interceptors to a subset of dispatches.

## Coverage

| Interface | `HandleAsync` |
|---|---|
| `ICommandPreInterceptor` | `ValueTask<object>(ICommand, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand>` | `ValueTask<TCommand>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` |
| `ICommandPreInterceptor<TCommand, TModified>` | `ValueTask<TModified>(TCommand, IExecutionContext)` |

The non-generic form intercepts any command, so it returns `object`. The single-parameter
form knows the command type and returns it — a pre-interceptor carries no result, so the
message is the only thing there is to return. The two-parameter form returns a different,
derived type.

Queries and events offer the same three shapes as `IQueryPreInterceptor…` and
`IEventPreInterceptor…`.

Next: [Post-interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/cross-cutting-concerns/post-interceptors).

This walks through a minimal but complete setup: one command, one query, one event, and the
startup code that registers all of them without a single hand-written registration call.

## Step 1 — Install

```bash
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare --prerelease
dotnet add package Stella.Ergosfare.SourceGenerator --prerelease
```

## Step 2 — Declare a command and its handler

A command is a message that *does* something. It may or may not produce a result; use
`ICommand<TResult>` when it does.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Abstractions;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions;

public sealed record CreateProduct(string Name, decimal Price) : ICommand<Guid>;

public sealed class CreateProductHandler : ICommandHandler<CreateProduct, Guid>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public CreateProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Guid> HandleAsync(CreateProduct command, IExecutionContext context)
        => _store.AddAsync(command.Name, command.Price, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

Two things worth noticing:

- The handler returns **`ValueTask<TResult>`**. A handler that completes synchronously
  allocates nothing.
- The cancellation token arrives on the **context**, not as a separate parameter. There is
  no way to forget to thread it through.

## Step 3 — Declare a query and its handler

A query *asks* something and always produces a result.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Abstractions;

public sealed record GetProduct(Guid Id) : IQuery<Product?>;

public sealed class GetProductHandler : IQueryHandler<GetProduct, Product?>
{
    private readonly IProductStore _store;

    public GetProductHandler(IProductStore store) => _store = store;

    public ValueTask<Product?> HandleAsync(GetProduct query, IExecutionContext context)
        => _store.FindAsync(query.Id, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Step 4 — Declare an event and its handlers

An event is broadcast: every matching handler runs, and there may be none.

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Abstractions;

public sealed record ProductCreated(Guid Id, string Name) : IEvent;

public sealed class SendWelcomeEmail : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _mailer.SendAsync(@event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}

public sealed class UpdateSearchIndex : IEventHandler<ProductCreated>
{
    public ValueTask HandleAsync(ProductCreated @event, IExecutionContext context)
        => _index.AddAsync(@event.Id, @event.Name, context.CancellationToken);
}
```

## Step 5 — Register everything

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Commands.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Queries.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Events.Extensions.MicrosoftDependencyInjection;
using Stella.Ergosfare.Generated;

var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

builder.Services.AddErgosfare(o => o
    .AddCommandModule(c => c.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddQueryModule(q => q.RegisterGenerated())
    .AddEventModule(e => e.RegisterGenerated()));
```

That is the whole registration. `RegisterGenerated()` does not exist in any NuGet package —
the source generator emits it into *your* compilation, already containing every handler it
found. Add a handler anywhere in the project and the next build picks it up.

{% aside type="tip" %}
`using Stella.Ergosfare.Generated;` is required — that is the namespace the generator emits
into. A missing `RegisterGenerated()` is almost always a missing using.
{% /aside %}

## Step 6 — Dispatch

Inject the mediator interface for the module you need. They are separate on purpose: a
component that only reads does not get the ability to send commands.

```csharp
app.MapPost("/products", async (
    CreateProduct command,
    ICommandMediator commands,
    IEventMediator events) =>
{
    var id = await commands.SendAsync(command);
    await events.PublishAsync(new ProductCreated(id, command.Name));
    return Results.Created($"/products/{id}", id);
});

app.MapGet("/products/{id:guid}", async (Guid id, IQueryMediator queries) =>
{
    var product = await queries.QueryAsync(new GetProduct(id));
    return product is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(product);
});
```

| Module | Interface | Call |
|---|---|---|
| Commands | `ICommandMediator` | `SendAsync(command)` |
| Queries | `IQueryMediator` | `QueryAsync(query)`, `StreamAsync(streamQuery)` |
| Events | `IEventMediator` | `PublishAsync(@event)` |

## Step 7 — Add a cross-cutting concern

Interceptors attach to the pipeline without touching handlers. A pre-interceptor registered
against a base type or interface applies to every message assignable to it:

```csharp
using Stella.Ergosfare.Core.Abstractions.Attributes;

[Weight(100)]                                  // higher weight runs earlier
public sealed class LogCommands : ICommandPreInterceptor
{
    private readonly ILogger<LogCommands> _logger;

    public LogCommands(ILogger<LogCommands> logger) => _logger = logger;

    public ValueTask<object> HandleAsync(ICommand command, IExecutionContext context)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Dispatching {Command}", command.GetType().Name);
        return ValueTask.FromResult<object>(command);   // pass the message through
    }
}
```

No registration needed — the generator finds it exactly like a handler.

{% aside type="note" %}
A pre-interceptor returns the message rather than `void`, because it is allowed to *rewrite*
it: whatever you return is what the next stage sees. Returning the message unchanged, as
above, is the pass-through case. See
[Interceptors](/ergosfare.docs/preview/core-concepts/interceptors).
{% /aside %}

